# This repo is worked on with core.autocrlf=true, which means git decides on a
# per-file basis whether something is text and rewrites its line endings on
# checkout. For binaries that is corruption, and the one that nearly bit was
# public/templates/workflows_cheatsheet.pdf: it happens to contain no CR bytes,
# so git classified it as text and would have injected CRLF into it on a fresh
# Windows clone — a 19 kB PDF that no longer opens. The .docx files were
# detected as binary only because their zip container has high-bit bytes early
# on; that is luck, not a guarantee.
#
# So: say it explicitly. Anything a browser downloads or a runtime parses byte
# for byte is binary.
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ico   binary

# Notebooks are JSON and diff usefully as text, but they must not be
# line-ending-rewritten: Colab and nbformat both read them as UTF-8 with LF,
# and a CRLF copy changes every line of every diff.
*.ipynb text eol=lf

# Course data and content: LF everywhere, so a checkout on Windows produces the
# same bytes the notebooks and the validator were tested against.
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.mdx   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
